end)
onPlayerKillCallback(function(world, player, killedPlayer) end)
live
Fires from world:kill(), the only PVP-style kill the engine has (its own deaths are environmental). killedPlayer is passed as both the second and third argument; no separate killer is known here.

onPlayerKillCallback(function(world, player, killedPlayer)
    print(killedPlayer:getCleanName() .. " was killed")
end)
onTick(function() end)
live
Fires about once a second. Cheap when unused. A handler that runs longer than its 1000ms budget is aborted rather than allowed to stack up.

local n = 0
onTick(function()
    n = n + 1
    if n % 60 == 0 then print("a minute passed") end
end)
onAutoSaveRequest(function() end)
live
Fires every 60 seconds as a "save your data now" prompt. It is a periodic tick, not a signal that a save is happening right then.

onAutoSaveRequest(function()
    saveMyState()
end)
onPlayerTick(function(player) end)
live
Runs about once per second for each online player. Keep the work light, since it fires for everyone on every tick.

onPlayerTick(function(player)
    -- runs about once per second, per player
end)
onPlayerPutOutFireCallback(function(world, player, tile) end)
live
Fires when a player puts a fire out. tile is the block that was burning. Advisory: the fire is already out by the time this runs, so the return value is not used. Use it to reward the player.

onPlayerPutOutFireCallback(function(world, player, tile)
    player:addGems(50)
    player:onConsoleMessage("`2Thanks for putting that out!")
end)
onBlacklistAccessCallback(function(player) end) → boolean [, string]
live
Gates No-/Buy items across buying, spawning and dropping. Registering it puts your script in charge of all of them. Once per action, not per item. Unregistered, the built-in rule applies.

return true
Allowed. If several callbacks are registered, any one returning true allows.
return false
Denied, and the command stays silent.
return false, message
Denied, and the message is shown to the player. Up to 512 bytes.
onBlacklistAccessCallback(function(player)
    if player:hasRole(998) then return true end
    return false, "`4Only staff can buy this."
end)
Globals
Callable from anywhere, with no prefix.

print(...)
live
Writes to the server log, prefixed [lua]. There is no client output.

require(name) → any
live
Loads another script from your server's script folder by name, without the .lua extension, and returns its value.

registerLuaCommand{ command, roleRequired, description, callback }
live
Declares a command so it is listed and echoed like a built-in.

command
Name without the slash.
roleRequired
Declarative only: stored and reported, never enforced, so keep your own hasRole check. An admin can also reassign the command to a role from in game, and from then on it is gated like a built-in.
description
Shown in listings.
callback
Optional f(player, args), tried before onPlayerCommandCallback.
registerLuaCommand{
    command = "card",
    roleRequired = 0,
    description = "Open your Credit Card"
}
getItem(id) → Item or nil
live
Returns an item handle, or nil for an unknown id, so getItem(id) and getItem(id):getName() is safe.

getItemsCount() → number
live
Total number of registered items on the server. This is a count, not the highest id.

for id = 0, getItemsCount() - 1 do
    local it = getItem(id)
    if it and it:getName() ~= "" then
        -- ...
    end
end
getServerPlayers() → {Player}
live
Every player who is online and has finished logging in, as a 1-based array. Peers still logging in have no name yet and are excluded.

for _, p in ipairs(getServerPlayers()) do
    p:onConsoleMessage("`2Server restarting in 5 minutes.")
end
getWorlds() → {World}
live
The worlds currently loaded in memory, as a 1-based array of World objects. This is NOT every world on the server: a world is loaded when someone enters or creates it and is dropped again when it unloads, so a world nobody has touched since the last restart is simply absent. That is also the right set for anything reading live state, because dropped items, players and current tile contents only exist for a loaded world. Order follows the load cache, so it is arrival order rather than alphabetical, and it is not stable between calls. Key your results by world:getName(), never by position.

local function findDrops(itemID)
    local hits = {}
    for _, w in ipairs(getWorlds()) do
        for _, d in ipairs(w:getDroppedItems()) do
            if d:getItemID() == itemID then
                hits[#hits + 1] = {
                    world = w:getName(),
                    x     = math.floor(d:getPosX() / 32),
                    y     = math.floor(d:getPosY() / 32),
                    count = d:getItemCount(),
                }
            end
        end
    end
    return hits
end

onPlayerCommandCallback(function(world, player, cmd)
    local id = cmd:match("^/finddrop%s+(%d+)$")
    if not id then return false end

    local hits = findDrops(tonumber(id))
    player:onConsoleMessage("`2" .. #hits .. "`` result(s) in loaded worlds")
    for _, h in ipairs(hits) do
        player:onConsoleMessage("`o" .. h.world .. "`` at " .. h.x .. "," .. h.y .. " x" .. h.count)
    end
    return true
end)
getPlayer(userID) → Player or nil
extension
Finds an online player by the number getUserID() returns, so getPlayer(p:getUserID()) round-trips.

getPlayerByName(name) → Player or nil
live
Finds an online player by name, case-insensitively. It matches the engine's own lowercased name field.

getServerName() → string
live
The server_name from the server config, the same value the engine compares against its variant macros.

saveDataToServer(key, table) → boolean
live
Stores a table in the server's key-value store, JSON-encoded. Uses bound parameters, so script text never reaches SQL.

saveDataToServer("player_" .. player:getUserID(), { done = true, streak = 3 })
loadDataFromServer(key) → table or nil
live
Returns the stored value decoded back into a table, or nil when the key is absent.

local d = loadDataFromServer("player_" .. player:getUserID())
if d and type(d) == "table" and d.done then
    -- ...
end
saveStringToServer(key, data) → boolean
live
The string-flavoured sibling of saveDataToServer: same store, same table, but the value is stored verbatim instead of being JSON-encoded. Max 1,000,000 bytes.

loadStringFromServer(key) → string or nil
live
Returns the raw stored string, or nil when the key is absent.

sqlite.open(file) → db
live
Opens a database in the server's folder. Handles expose db:query(sql) and db:close(). WAL, 2s busy timeout. Unclosed handles are freed on reload.

local db = sqlite.open("mine.db")
local rows = db:query("SELECT a, b FROM t") or {}
db:close()
json.encode(value) → string
live
Encodes a Lua value to JSON, using the same encoder saveDataToServer uses.

local s = json.encode({ name = "brighver", level = 4 })
json.decode(string) → value or nil
live
Decodes JSON to a Lua value, or nil on malformed input; never raises. json.save and json.all are from other engines and are not part of this global.

registerLuaPlaymod(modData) → number
live
Registers a script-defined playmod and returns its id for addMod(id). All optional: iconID, modName, onAddMessage, onRemoveMessage, changeSkin = {r,g,b,a}.

local id = registerLuaPlaymod({ modName = "Frost", iconID = 274, changeSkin = {150,200,255,255} })
player:addMod(id, 0)

-- Built in id worth knowing: 203 "Grumbleteeth" replaces what the
-- player says with mumbled syllables. They can still talk.
player:addMod(203, 600)   -- 10 minutes of mumbling
addSidebarButton(jsonObject)
live
Adds a sidebar button from a JSON string like {"active":true,"name":"MyButton",...}. Baked into the login packet, so it shows on first menu open.

addSidebarButton(json.encode({
    active = true, name = "ProfitButton", buttonAction = "open_profit",
    buttonTemplate = "BaseEventButton", itemIdIcon = 14404,
    order = 50, rcssClass = "daily_challenge", text = "Profit"
}))
timer.setTimeout(seconds, fn) → number
live
Runs fn once, about `seconds` from now. Accurate to roughly a second, not millisecond-accurate. The delay is clamped to 0 through 3600. Useful for deferring work until after a callback returns. Returns a handle you can pass to timer.clearInterval to cancel it before it fires.

onPlayerRegisterCallback(function(world, player)
    timer.setTimeout(10, function()
        if player:isOnline() then showWelcome(player) end
    end)
end)
timer.setInterval(seconds, fn) → number
live
Runs fn over and over, about every `seconds`, until you stop it. Returns a handle for timer.clearInterval. Keep that handle: without it the callback cannot be stopped short of reloading the scripts. The period is clamped to 1 through 3600 seconds. The floor is 1 because timers are only checked about once a second, so a smaller number would promise a rate the server cannot deliver. If a callback throws, the error is logged and the interval keeps running, and if the server is busy enough to miss several periods you get one call rather than a burst of catch-up calls.

seconds
How long between runs. Clamped to 1 through 3600.
fn
Called with no arguments. Errors inside it are logged and do not stop the interval.
-- announce a giveaway every 5 minutes, and stop once it has run
local announcer

local function startGiveaway()
    local left = 3
    announcer = timer.setInterval(300, function()
        left = left - 1
        for _, p in ipairs(getAllPlayers()) do
            p:onConsoleMessage("`2Giveaway`` starting soon!")
        end
        if left <= 0 then
            timer.clearInterval(announcer)
            announcer = nil
        end
    end)
end
timer.clearInterval(id) → boolean
live
Stops a timer by the handle setInterval returned, and returns whether one was actually stopped. It also cancels a setTimeout that has not fired yet, since both live in the same list. Safe to call from inside a timer callback, including on the interval that is currently running and on another timer due in the same moment. Clearing an id twice, or an id that never existed, returns false rather than failing.

