tile:getTileData(index) → number or nil
live
Reads a tile's growth data from a world:getTiles() tile. index 0 is the fruit count and only trees carry it. index 1 is the start timestamp, carried by trees and by providers alike, and both are ready once os.time() minus that value reaches the item's getGrowTime(). Returns nil when the tile is not one of those types, so a dirt block or a lock never answers with a misleading 0, and nil is a valid "is this a tree" test. A 0 you do get back is a real 0.

index
0 for the fruit count (trees only), 1 for the planted or provider cycle-start timestamp (trees and providers).
for _, t in ipairs(world:getTiles()) do
    local fruit = t:getTileData(0)
    if fruit and fruit > 0 then
        player:onConsoleMessage("Tree at " .. t:getPosX() .. "," .. t:getPosY() .. " has " .. fruit .. " fruit.")
    end

    -- index 1 is shared by trees and providers, so one check covers both
    local started = t:getTileData(1)
    if started then
        local left = started + getItem(t:getTileID()):getGrowTime() - os.time()
        if left <= 0 then
            player:onConsoleMessage("`2Ready`` at " .. t:getPosX() .. "," .. t:getPosY())
        end
    end
end
tile:setTileData(index, value) → boolean
live
Writes tile data on a tile from world:getTiles(). index 0 is the fruit count, clamped to 0..255 and accepted by trees only. index 1 is the planted or provider cycle-start timestamp, accepted by trees and providers. Gated exactly like tile:getTileData, so what you can read back is what you can write. Returns false on a read-only snapshot tile from a break or wrench callback, and on any tile whose type does not carry that value: a Giving Tree, for instance, keeps a future deadline in the same field and would be broken by a write meant for a tree.

tile:setTileDataInt(index, value) → boolean
live
The same as tile:setTileData. Both names are accepted.

